  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>A tanker registered under a Panama flag sustained a projectile strike in the eastern Gulf on the evening of June 27, per UKMO maritime alerts and Reuters. At roughly the same hour, IRGC coastal forces refused a US Hormuz deconfliction channel established under the June 21–22 Bürgenstock framework. Lloyd's Market Association raised the war-risk exclusion zone to cover transit lanes within 12 nautical miles of the Iranian coast. IMF PortWatch recorded ≤5 commercial transits through the full strait on June 27, with live AIS trackers showing 4 vessels active on June 28. → moved <span class="cite">F4</span></p>

      <p>IRGC confirmed three ballistic strikes against US military infrastructure in Kuwait (Camp Arifjan) and Bahrain (NSA Bahrain) beginning 04:17 UTC June 28, per Reuters and AP. CENTCOM reported two of three missiles intercepted by Patriot batteries; a third caused structural damage to an outlying logistics building at Camp Arifjan with no US fatalities confirmed at time of publication. IRGC attributed the strikes to US "co-belligerence" in MCM operations and "facilitation of Israeli asset protection." → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>United Torah Judaism's two Knesset factions formally departed Israel's governing coalition on June 28 over Haredi conscription legislation (i24NEWS, Times of Israel). The coalition fell to 60 seats — below the 61-seat majority threshold. A dissolution bill passed first reading 106-0 with Netanyahu's publicly declared support; Netanyahu subsequently announced pursuit of a "broad national unity government" after elections without specifying a date or a coalition partner. → moved <span class="cite">F6</span>, <span class="cite">F7</span></p>

      <p>Pakistani and Qatari mediators issued separate statements on June 28 confirming the Bürgenstock framework "remained in effect" and that the 60-day technical negotiation roadmap had not been formally suspended. Pezeshkian's office confirmed via ISNA that the president "remains committed to the Islamabad MOU." No named US or Iranian representative had publicly confirmed or denied re-scheduling of technical working group sessions as of this publication. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 4. SCENARIO MAP ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="scenario-map">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Scenario Map
      <span class="meta">Computed from Forecast Board · 1 sentence per scenario</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="scenario-map">
      <div class="prob-bar-container">
        <div class="prob-bar prob-escalation" style="width:50%;">Escalation 50%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-status" style="width:35%;">Protracted 35%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-deescalation" style="width:15%;">De-esc 15%</div>
      </div>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path:</strong> IRGC strikes on US bases (June 28) and the tanker attack (June 27) — with the Hormuz deconfliction hotline refused — constitute a kinetic escalation pattern that a US military response could convert into open conflict, collapsing the Bürgenstock roadmap and N2 (45%) technical-talk track.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path:</strong> MOU and Bürgenstock framework nominally survive (Pakistani/Qatari mediators confirm June 28), but IRGC-Pezeshkian internal contradictions produce a frozen conflict with periodic violence and no formal ceasefire — Hormuz stays at 4–20 transits/day (NFR-2 = 18%) and technical talks stall without resolution.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path:</strong> A US decision not to respond militarily to the June 28 IRGC strikes, combined with N2 (45%) technical talks reconvening by July 12 and IRGC standing down the Hormuz closure declaration, returns the situation to the Bürgenstock sequence — the narrow prerequisite chain for NFR-2 to resolve YES.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>IRNA and Press TV carried two distinct editorial threads on June 27–28 without acknowledging their contradiction: one treated the Islamabad MOU and Bürgenstock roadmap as "a diplomatic victory of the Islamic Republic's principled resistance"; the second, drawing on IRGC citations, framed the tanker strike and base attacks as "legitimate and measured self-defense against Zionist-American aggression." The two framings ran on the same outlets within the same 24-hour window. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>Tasnim published, on June 28 and without editorial cross-reference, two substantively incompatible pieces: one quoting unnamed Majlis deputies praising the "historic potential of the 60-day Bürgenstock roadmap," a second quoting IRGC Navy Commander Rear Admiral Tangsiri on the "measured legal response" to MCM operations in the strait. Kayhan's editorial board called for "complete Hormuz closure until all American naval vessels withdraw from the Persian Gulf region" — a demand absent from any MFA or Pezeshkian-government statement. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>Israeli coalition collapse received prominent front-page coverage in Kayhan, Fars, and IRNA on June 28, framed as confirmation of "the structural collapse of the Zionist entity under the blows of the united resistance." None of the three outlets attributed the coalition's fall to the domestic Haredi military-service dispute; the UTJ conscription context was absent from the IRGC-adjacent outlets entirely, present only in IRNA English service's parallel factual account. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

